---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CurrentViews from '../components/CurrentViews.astro';
import { milestones, laterMilestones, site } from '../data/site';
---

<Layout
  title="Progress"
  section="Progress"
  description="Locally accepted kernel and hosted UI progress, measured hosted observations, and the next ContinuumOS milestones."
>
  <section class="page-intro wrap">
    <span class="section-label">THE PROGRESS / REVIEWED {site.reviewed}</span>
    <h1>
      A foundation that boots.
      <br />
      <span class="mint">A UI that continues.</span>
    </h1>
    <p class="lead">
      M1’s experimental QEMU kernel baseline and M2’s Windows-hosted UI subset pass local
      acceptance. A usable guest desktop and guest live updates remain ahead.
    </p>
    <p class="muted">
      Results through {site.resultsThrough}. Acceptance is local implementation/self-review, not
      independent approval.
    </p>
  </section>
  <div class="page-content wrap progress-content">
    <h2 class="sr-only">Current execution environments</h2>
    <CurrentViews />
    <section id="roadmap" class="roadmap-section" aria-labelledby="roadmap-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <span class="section-label">THE ROADMAP / GATES, NOT DATES</span>
          <h2 id="roadmap-title">
            From foundations
            <br />
            to a coherent release.
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p class="muted">
          Technical dependencies set the order. A locally accepted milestone closes its declared
          scope; it does not accept later capabilities.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="milestones">
        {milestones.map((m) => (
          <article
            class:list={[
              'milestone',
              {
                'milestone-accepted': m.status === 'Locally accepted',
                'milestone-next': m.id === 'M3',
              },
            ]}
            id={m.id.toLowerCase()}
          >
            <a
              class="milestone-id"
              href={`#${m.id.toLowerCase()}`}
              aria-label={`Link to milestone ${m.id}`}
            >
              {m.id}
            </a>
            <div>
              <div class="milestone-meta">
                <span class:list={['pill', { planned: m.status === 'Planned' }]}>
                  {m.status} — {m.scope}
                </span>
                <span class="micro">{m.environment}</span>
              </div>
              <h3>{m.title}</h3>
              <p>{m.description}</p>
              <small class="muted">Reviewed {m.reviewed}</small>
            </div>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="later-section" aria-labelledby="later-title">
      <span class="section-label">BEYOND THE EXPERIMENTAL RELEASE</span>
      <h2 id="later-title">
        A wider horizon.
        <br />
        Separate proof.
      </h2>
      <div class="later-grid">
        {laterMilestones.map((m) => (
          <article id={m.id}>
            <span class="index">{m.range} / PLANNED</span>
            <h3>{m.title}</h3>
            <p>{m.text}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>
    <section id="performance" class="performance-section" aria-labelledby="performance-title">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <div>
          <span class="section-label">HOSTED OBSERVATIONS / OCTOBER 24, 2026</span>
          <h2 id="performance-title">
            Measure the work.
            <br />
            <span class="mint">Keep the misses visible.</span>
          </h2>
        </div>
        <p class="muted">
          The bounded hosted rendering/publication follow-up passes local regression. These elapsed
          host timings do not accept guest performance targets.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="metric-grid">
        <article>
          <span class="micro">1080p FILE RELOAD / P95</span>
          <strong>
            128.116 <small>ms</small>
          </strong>
          <h3>Completed build → RAM</h3>
          <p>
            100 actual file reloads. Includes debounce, validation, staging and RAM submission.
            Compilation and native display latency are excluded.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="micro">RICH TYPING + SEARCH / P95</span>
          <strong>
            18.642 <small>ms</small>
          </strong>
          <h3>Frame period exceeded</h3>
          <p>Elapsed frame time remains above the 16.667 ms period for this workload.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="micro">RICH EFFECTS + MOTION / P95</span>
          <strong>
            17.424 <small>ms</small>
          </strong>
          <h3>Frame period exceeded</h3>
          <p>Elapsed frame time remains above the 16.667 ms period for this workload.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <p class="review-note">
        Frame observations: 1920 × 1080, scale 1, tracing off; 1,000 samples per workload/effect
        cell across five fresh processes. Timings include host scheduling. RAM submission is not
        scanout or physical input latency. Renderer-thread CPU and guest input targets remain
        unaccepted.
      </p>
      <div class="memory-target">
        <span class="target-value">
          256 <span>MiB</span>
        </span>
        <div>
          <span class="pill planned">Unmet target · not a measurement</span>
          <h3>Cold-idle guest memory</h3>
          <p>
            The whole-guest target includes kernel, services, app shells, framebuffers and bounded
            caches. Hosted process memory does not establish it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="article-block">
      <span class="section-label">THE NEXT CONTINUITY PROOF</span>
      <h2>Bring the result into the guest.</h2>
      <p>
        M3 first establishes isolated execution and authority boundaries. M4 must then prove guest
        resource changes with unsaved work preserved and unchanged kernel boot identity and uptime.
        M5 separately proves application behavior replacement and safe retirement of old execution.
      </p>
      <p>
        The current hosted result changes resources within the same process and window. The browser
        playground illustrates later possibilities; it cannot supply guest evidence.
      </p>
    </section>
    <div class="note">
      <h3>Curated progress, bounded claims.</h3>
      <p>
        Public summaries describe retained internal results. Private source, raw logs and binaries
        are not published here. There is no public OS download or promised release date.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
